          # Slope approximation dx2_dt has length N-1. dx2_dt[i] is slope       
  between i and i+1.                                                            
          # If transition happens at t, we look at dx2_dt[t-1] (slope ending    
  at t) and dx2_dt[t] (slope starting at t).                                    
                                                                                
          # Need to handle boundary conditions for t=0, 1, N-1. Since t is      
  from transition_times >= 2, t-1 >= 1.                                         
                                                                                
          slope_before = dx2_dt[t-1] # Slope leading up to time t               
          slope_after = dx2_dt[t] if t < N - 1 else np.nan # Slope starting     
  from time t                                                                   
                                                                                
          # The d2x2_dt2 index corresponding to the change ending at t is i =   
  t-2.                                                                          
          # d2x2_dt2[t-2] = slope_after - slope_before, if dx2_dt was           
  calculated using [t] - [t-1] for the second diff.                             
          # Let's check indices carefully:                                      
          # dx2_dt[i] = x2[i+1] - x2[i]                                         
          # d2x2_dt2[j] = dx2_dt[j+1] - dx2_dt[j]                               
          # If transition_time t=2:                                             
          # d2x2_dt2[0] = dx2_dt[1] - dx2_dt[0]                                 
          # dx2_dt[0] is slope(0->1), dx2_dt[1] is slope(1->2). The change      
  occurs between t=1 and t=2.                                                   
          # So, the transition point t = 2 is the first time step where the     
  new behavior (slope_after) is observed compared to the old behavior           
  (slope_before).                                                               
          # slope_before = dx2_dt[t-2] (Slope from t-2 to t-1) if we consider   
  the abrupt change is caused by the state at t.                                
          # Let's rely on: slope_before is the last stable slope, slope_after   
  is the first slope of the new mode.                                           
                                                                                
          # Slope ending at t (i.e., between t-1 and t)                         
          slope_prev_mode = dx2_dt[t-1]                                         
          # Slope starting at t (i.e., between t and t+1)                       
          slope_new_mode = dx2_dt[t] if t < N - 1 else np.nan                   
                                                                                
                                                                                
          detailed_results.append({                                             
              'Time_t': tp['t'],                                                
              'State_at_t': (tp['x1'], tp['x2']),                               
              'Slope_ending_at_t_minus_1': slope_prev_mode,                     
